speechtext:
1. IDRC does not itself conduct research on birth control methods. It does, however, provide funds to other institutions for such research.
2.	From 1971 to the end of 1978, $10,322,621.61 was committed by the centre to research projects in the following areas:
(a)	development of new and improved contraceptive methods;
(b)	short-time clinical trials to assess safety and acceptance of different contraceptive methods;
(c)	long-term assessment of safety of contraceptive methods in different populations;
(d)	small grants to researchers in the field of human reproduction following their return to developing country institutions after a period of specialized training outside their own countries; and,
(e)	studies on family planning program administration, improved contraceptive distribution systems, and the use of different personnel for the delivery of family planning services.
3.	Such expenditures do appear in the centre's annual reports.
